WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.575
Servers and cloud machines don't
follow the endpoint pattern.

2
00:00:03.575 --> 00:00:06.336
On premises, servers with fixed addresses

3
00:00:06.336 --> 00:00:09.413
get static records in
the IPAM and DDI system.

4
00:00:09.413 --> 00:00:12.295
Static records aren't
removed by scavenging,

5
00:00:12.295 --> 00:00:15.100
the cleanup job that
deletes stale entries.

6
00:00:15.100 --> 00:00:18.970
As program design guidance,
the build pipeline that creates

7
00:00:18.970 --> 00:00:22.762
a server should also create and
delete its A and PTR records.

8
00:00:22.762 --> 00:00:27.690
Linux servers in an identity domain
can register themselves with GSS-TSIG,

9
00:00:27.690 --> 00:00:32.061
but watch for one documented
behavior: if signing fails at install,

10
00:00:32.061 --> 00:00:35.399
the client falls back to
unauthenticated updates.

11
00:00:35.399 --> 00:00:38.961
For Azure Arc, set the
resource name to match the DNS

12
00:00:38.961 --> 00:00:42.437
name at connect time, because
it can't change later.

13
00:00:42.437 --> 00:00:47.111
In Azure, private DNS autoregistration
does the job, with limits:

14
00:00:47.111 --> 00:00:52.489
virtual machines only, the primary
network interface only, A records only,

15
00:00:52.489 --> 00:00:57.338
no PTR records, and one autoregistration
zone per virtual network.

16
00:00:57.338 --> 00:01:00.603
Records disappear when the
VM is deleted or stopped,

17
00:01:00.603 --> 00:01:05.237
deleted records can't be restored, and
unlinking the network deletes them.

18
00:01:05.237 --> 00:01:09.262
In AWS, instances get AWS-made names.

19
00:01:09.262 --> 00:01:14.939
An AWS blog from 2016 says VPC DNS
doesn't create records dynamically,

20
00:01:14.939 --> 00:01:18.425
and offers a sample event-driven pipeline.

21
00:01:18.425 --> 00:01:24.650
I found no built-in registration into
your own Route 53 zones in AWS's docs.

22
00:01:24.650 --> 00:01:32.380
Sources: Microsoft Learn: DNS aging and
scavenging [S30], azcmagent connect [S15],

23
00:01:32.380 --> 00:01:40.714
Rename Azure Arc-enabled servers [S16],
VirtualMachineOSProfile.ComputerName [S17],

24
00:01:40.714 --> 00:01:48.566
Azure DNS private zones autoregistration
[S20]; FreeIPA ipa-client-install [S14];

25
00:01:48.566 --> 00:01:56.054
AWS: EC2 instance hostname types [S21],
Working with private hosted zones [S22],

26
00:01:56.054 --> 00:02:02.094
AWS Compute Blog, Building a Dynamic
DNS for Route 53 (2016) [S23];

27
00:02:02.094 --> 00:02:08.060
the Federal Modernization
Workbook's Book 08 Ch07 and Ch10.
